1. When there is multi-master communication, then only arbitration comes into picture.

2. In the I2C arbitration zero is dominant bit and 1 is recessive bit.

3. The master which will write first 0, becomes winner in arbitration.

4. I2C bus is "Wired AND bus"

	Arbitration only works when the 2 masters are sending data at the same time 

5. In the I2C arbitration, each transmitted bit is monitored by I2C bus master

	m1 = 1010 0000 		m2 = 1100 0000

	Here, the 2 masters m1 and m2 have send the first bit "1"
	
	The bus voltage now is 3.3v 

	After this both master's m1 & m2 will read the current status of the bus and compare it with the first bit it have send.

	If both are equal, then only the master will continue to send the next bit

	Now m1 and m2 have send 0 and m2 have send 1. Now the bus status is ( 0 & 1 = 0)

	Now the both masters compares the bus status with the bit send, so now here the winner is m1 
	
	If one master get lost, arbitration lost condition will connection. The lost master and m2 will wait till the winner master generates the stop condition.

	Atlast the slave will check the received slave address, and only the slave which have same slave address will only produce ACK.

	So the other slaves will wait till the next start condition is generated. 

	

When both masters have same SA
------------------------------

Eg : M1 : SA + W -> 1010 0000  
    				 both are winner (arbitration)
     M2 : SA + W -> 1010 0000


     M1 m/r  -> 0000 0001
				(no arbitration)
     M2 m/r  -> 0000 0010 	
	
     (0000 0001 & 0000 0010) = 0000 0000 (wrong data, no err detection in i2c)
					 (corrupted m/r address)

     M1 data -> 0100 0001 
				(no arbitration)
     M2 data -> 0100 0010 		
	
    (0100 0001 & 0100 0010) = 0100 0000 (wrong data, no error detection in i2c)
					(corrupted data)




     Note 
     ----

	* When both masters are winner, data gets corrupted as well as memory address gets corrupted.

	* When same data and memory address is sending then no corruption takes place.










































